export const WRITEUPS = {"alv": {"slug": "alv", "name": "CASE × Ama La Vida", "floor": "analytics", "onSite": true, "order": 1, "template": "analytics", "status": "approved", "oneLiner": "Pricing and retention strategy for a virtual career-coaching firm, read from its members' plan changes.", "context": {"course": "", "when": "Winter 2025", "team": "Six analysts, two project managers", "partner": "Ama La Vida, through CASE (Consulting Advising Student Enterprise)", "role": "Strategy Analyst — pricing & retention"}, "software": ["r", "sheets", "slides"], "skills": [{"name": "Problem framing", "evidence": "approach"}, {"name": "Statistics", "evidence": "approach"}, {"name": "Data visualisation", "evidence": "analysis"}, {"name": "Pricing strategy", "evidence": "recommendation"}, {"name": "Client communication", "evidence": "recommendation"}], "classes": [], "cover": "assets/projects/alv.webp", "blocks": [{"id": "question", "stage": "question", "title": "Raise prices without pushing members out", "text": "Ama La Vida (ALV) sells one-on-one career coaching over video, as memberships. In January 2025 its founders brought CASE, a Northwestern student consulting group, three asks: benchmark what comparable coaching costs, recommend new prices to be in place by June, and plan how to tell current members.\n\nWe split into three workstreams — competitors, pricing & retention, and rollout. I was one of three analysts on pricing & retention: the half of the question about keeping members once the prices change.", "layout": "text", "media": []}, {"id": "context", "stage": "context", "title": "The cheapest of four similar coaching firms", "text": "ALV coaches for long-range career direction rather than résumé fixes, and priced by plan: two sessions a month for $379 — $189.50 a session — or 12- and 24-session bundles at $1,933 and $3,638. Our competitor workstream found three firms with a similar pitch charging $225 to $450 a session, so there was room to charge more.\n\nThe catch: at every renewal, a member can continue, stop, or downgrade to one session a month or a cheaper community plan.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/alv/competitor-prices.webp", "caption": "From the final client deck: ALV against three firms with a similar pitch (competitor workstream's slide).", "kind": "screen"}]}, {"id": "data", "stage": "data", "title": "Two datasets, one arriving mid-project", "text": "We worked from ALV's own records. First, cancellation files from 2020 to 2024, each with the reason the member gave. Then, in mid-February, an export of membership history: every move from one plan to another, more than 2,000 of them. I did my analysis in R.\n\nThe files needed work before they could talk — plan names typed inconsistently, some members missing a start date — and the revenue-by-product data our workstream requested in January never shows as received in the request tracker.", "layout": "text", "media": []}, {"id": "approach", "stage": "approach", "title": "Follow the downgrades, not just the cancellations", "text": "My first task was to test, with a small churn model, whether the reasons members gave for cancelling tracked the plan they were on. Useful for retention, but it couldn't settle price: a cancellation says someone left, not how much of the plan they still wanted.\n\nSo when the plan-change history arrived in February, I turned to the members who hadn't quit: when they switched, in which direction, and to what. A downgrade is a member telling you what a plan is worth to them — the price signal cancellations can't give.", "layout": "text", "media": []}, {"id": "analysis", "stage": "analysis", "title": "Plan changes ran one way: down", "text": "The history was lopsided: downgrades outnumbered upgrades and unchanged renewals many times over. And the moves went down the ladder, mostly to the virtual plan — 69% of changes out of the one-month subscription, 75% out of 2×-monthly and 83% out of 1×-monthly ended there, while 55% of changes out of 4×-monthly stepped down to 2×.\n\nOur project manager read my first charts and asked for exactly those percentages; my charts on where members moved became two slides of the final client deck.", "layout": "gallery", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/alv/plan-changes-direction.webp", "caption": "Final client deck: every plan change by type.", "kind": "chart"}, {"src": "assets/projects/alv/plan-changes-length.webp", "caption": "Where members moved by plan length — my chart, restyled for the deck.", "kind": "chart"}, {"src": "assets/projects/alv/plan-changes-frequency.webp", "caption": "Where members moved by session frequency — my chart, restyled for the deck.", "kind": "chart"}]}, {"id": "recommendation", "stage": "recommendation", "title": "Raise the ceiling, and reward staying up", "text": "The final deck joined two findings. From the benchmark: a $250 ceiling per session with the old bundle discounts kept, taking 12 sessions from $1,933 to $2,550 and 24 from $3,638 to $4,800. From my workstream: since members trade down, make staying up worth it — existing members keep their price by renewing for 12 months; new members get bigger discounts for more sessions and longer terms.\n\nMy own notes had argued for changing discounts instead of raising every price. The team did both.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/alv/bundle-prices-old-new.webp", "caption": "The recommended ceiling and bundle prices (final client deck).", "kind": "screen"}, {"src": "assets/projects/alv/two-step-plan-picker.webp", "caption": "The plan picker we proposed for ALV's website: choose a frequency, then a length.", "kind": "mockup"}]}, {"id": "next", "stage": "next", "title": "Test the ceiling on members, not rivals", "text": "Two things I'd do differently. The $250 ceiling rests on four firms' list prices, not on how ALV's own members respond; the phased rollout we proposed — loyal members first, then everyone — could double as the price test, if downgrades are tracked cohort by cohort.\n\nAnd my early churn model deserved more suspicion. Every record in that file was a cancellation, so \"churn\" had to be defined from tenure — and tenure then \"predicted\" it. I'd rebuild it on what's known the day someone signs up.", "layout": "text", "media": []}]}, "amg": {"slug": "amg", "name": "AMG-786", "floor": "analytics", "onSite": true, "order": 3, "template": "analytics", "status": "approved", "oneLiner": "A go/no-go case for Amgen's oral obesity drug AMG-786: timeline, costs, price and NPV.", "context": {"course": "CIV_ENV 205 · Economics & Finance for Engineers", "when": "Winter 2024", "team": "Team of 5", "partner": "", "role": "Financial analyst — pricing and valuation"}, "software": ["excel", "slides"], "skills": [{"name": "Secondary research", "evidence": "data"}, {"name": "Statistics", "evidence": "approach"}, {"name": "Pricing strategy", "evidence": "analysis"}, {"name": "Excel modelling", "evidence": "analysis"}, {"name": "Financial modelling (NPV)", "evidence": "recommendation"}], "classes": ["civenv205"], "cover": "assets/projects/amg.webp", "blocks": [{"id": "question", "stage": "question", "title": "Should Amgen keep paying for an unproven pill?", "text": "In CIV_ENV 205, Economics and Finance for Engineers, each team spent the quarter building a go/no-go case for a drug still in development: its schedule, costs quarter by quarter, risks, sales, price and net present value. Ours was AMG-786, Amgen's small-molecule obesity drug, then in Phase I, taken by mouth, its mechanism undisclosed.\n\nThere were five of us, each with a role in the team contract. Mine was \"Calculator\": the person who works in Excel to find the numeric answers.", "layout": "text", "media": []}, {"id": "context", "stage": "context", "title": "A pill entering an injection gold rush", "text": "Obesity drugs were booming, and Amgen was behind. Novo Nordisk and Eli Lilly owned the market: in 2023 Ozempic sold $13.9 billion, Mounjaro $5.2 billion and Wegovy $4.5 billion — all injections. AMG-786's pitch was a pill.\n\nIts risks were just as specific. Amgen's own injectable, AMG-133, was a step ahead in Phase II and could undercut it; Pfizer had recently dropped a similar oral drug in Phase II because patients couldn't tolerate its side effects.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/amg/competitors.webp", "caption": "The field AMG-786 would enter (final deck).", "kind": "screen"}]}, {"id": "data", "stage": "data", "title": "Public averages for a drug without precedent", "text": "Amgen had published almost nothing about AMG-786, so every number came from outside: cost meta-analyses (DiMasi et al.'s 2016 sample of 106 drugs, a Nature analysis of the development funnel), phase-transition rates, competitors' annual reports and list prices, and a forecast of 14.6% yearly market growth to 2032.\n\nThe snag we kept hitting: public drug statistics report averages, not spreads. Only DiMasi gave standard deviations — and his were the most optimistic numbers we found.", "layout": "text", "media": []}, {"id": "approach", "stage": "approach", "title": "Budget for the tail, not the average", "text": "We chose to be pessimistic on purpose, then had to defend it. In week 5, DiMasi's confidence intervals came in far below our first estimates: his upper bound for Phase I was $30.9 million; we had budgeted $102 million. We kept the high numbers. Costs in every source were skewed right, with volatility growing phase by phase, and AMG-786 would be a first-of-its-kind oral tablet.\n\nPhase II got the same treatment: we cut the usual 61% pass rate to about 50%, and set the whole timeline at 105 months.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/amg/development-timeline.webp", "caption": "Our development timeline: 28 + 31 + 30 months of trials and 16 of FDA review.", "kind": "chart"}]}, {"id": "analysis", "stage": "analysis", "title": "My job: what should a month cost?", "text": "Pricing was my section. I took the monthly list prices of the four drugs AMG-786 would meet — Wegovy $1,349, Mounjaro $1,100, Zepbound $1,060, Ozempic $959.60 — and carried each forward at 3.1% inflation a year to 2033, when our 8.7-year timeline put AMG-786 on sale. They averaged $1,470.41, so I proposed $1,400 to $1,500 a month.\n\nAt $1,400, the team's first-year sales target of about $1 billion meant roughly 714,000 monthly doses, growing 11% a year to $3.37 billion in year ten.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/amg/pricing-2033.webp", "caption": "My pricing slide: 2024 list prices carried to 2033 at 3.1% inflation.", "kind": "chart"}]}, {"id": "recommendation", "stage": "recommendation", "title": "Proceed — but wary of Phase II", "text": "On a $1.36 billion investment and ten years of sales, our NPV came out strongly positive: $12.1 billion with 11% yearly growth (IRR 84%), $6.9 billion with none (IRR 73%).\n\nOur recommendation was more careful than those numbers. Proceed, but keep later-stage spending as small as possible until AMG-786 clears Phase II — the stage its closest rival had just failed.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/amg/revenue-and-npv.webp", "caption": "Projected revenue and the two NPV scenarios (final deck).", "kind": "chart"}]}, {"id": "next", "stage": "next", "title": "Our NPV forgot our own decision tree", "text": "Two fixes, one of them mine. Our \"risk-adjusted\" NPV varied only sales growth and assumed AMG-786 reached the market. The transition rates we cited put approval near 29%; our own decision tree, near 2%. Neither number touched the NPV.\n\nAnd my pricing section's sanity check set 714,000 monthly doses beside 4% of 16 million people. But a patient buys twelve doses a year, so $1 billion is about 60,000 patients. I'd weight the NPV by the tree and size demand in patients, not doses.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/amg/decision-tree.webp", "caption": "The decision tree (teammate's): 54% × 34% × 18.5% × 70% ≈ 2% to reach the market.", "kind": "diagram"}]}]}, "austin": {"slug": "austin", "name": "Austin Vaccine Siting", "floor": "analytics", "onSite": true, "order": 0, "template": "analytics", "status": "approved", "oneLiner": "Grouping Austin's 44 ZIP areas for vaccine planning: a clustering heuristic, then two integer programs.", "context": {"course": "IEMS 313 · Foundations of Optimization", "when": "Winter 2025", "team": "Team of 4 (Phase 1), 3 (Phase 2)", "partner": "", "role": "Optimization analyst — clustering and integer-programming models"}, "software": ["python", "jupyter", "colab", "pandas", "numpy", "sklearn", "gurobi"], "skills": [{"name": "Problem framing", "evidence": "question"}, {"name": "Python", "evidence": "approach"}, {"name": "Optimization (integer programming)", "evidence": "approach"}, {"name": "Clustering", "evidence": "approach"}, {"name": "Design criteria & decision matrices", "evidence": "analysis"}, {"name": "Data visualisation", "evidence": "analysis"}, {"name": "Technical writing", "evidence": "recommendation"}], "classes": ["iems313"], "cover": "assets/projects/austin.webp", "blocks": [{"id": "question", "stage": "question", "title": "Split a city fairly, three ways at once", "text": "Our brief was a public-health one: group Austin's 44 ZIP Code Tabulation Areas (ZCTAs) into two, three or four clusters that could each anchor vaccination planning. Every grouping had to keep socially vulnerable areas together, keep neighbours close and follow how people actually travel between areas — without handing one cluster most of the city's 1,146,600 people.\n\nSo the real question was never \"what is the best clustering?\" It was: how much of one good thing do you give up for another, and who gets to decide?", "layout": "text", "media": []}, {"id": "context", "stage": "context", "title": "Phase 1 allowed a heuristic; Phase 2 didn't", "text": "IEMS 313 (Foundations of Optimization) ran the project in two rounds. Phase 1 (January 2025): design our own clustering method — we were told at least one approach had to be original — and defend it in a report with a four-page limit. Phase 2 (March): solve the same problem as integer programs, using two objective functions the course gave us. A course virtual machine drew the maps. Four of us wrote Phase 1; three of us wrote Phase 2.", "layout": "text", "media": []}, {"id": "data", "stage": "data", "title": "Forty-four areas, three ways to measure distance", "text": "The course gave us three tables for Austin's 44 ZCTAs: population with latitude and longitude; a Social Vulnerability Index (SVI) score from 0 to 1; and connectivity — the log of average daily visits — for every pair of areas. Each became a 44 × 44 \"distance\": SVI as the gap between two scores, GEO as Haversine kilometres weighted by the pair's average population, CON as how far a pair's traffic falls below the busiest link. We MinMax-scaled all three so they could be added.\n\nWhat the files never say: where the SVI scores and visit counts come from, or which year they describe.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/austin/svi-heatmap.webp", "caption": "The map we started from: SVI by ZCTA, from the course's starter files. Darker = more vulnerable; the darkest areas sit on the east side.", "kind": "chart"}]}, {"id": "approach", "stage": "approach", "title": "A penalty first, then a hard floor", "text": "Plain k-medoids ignores population, so in Phase 1 we added a penalty to its cost: the squared gap between each cluster's population and an equal share. We ran it from hundreds of spread-out starting points and kept the best. We chose k-medoids over k-means because its centres are real ZIP areas.\n\nPhase 2 turned the penalty into a rule: every cluster holds at least 80% of an equal share. My own pass at that integer program — Pyomo with the free CBC solver — was still on K = 2 when I stopped it. The team's version ran in Gurobi, capped at two minutes or a 5% optimality gap.", "layout": "text", "media": []}, {"id": "analysis", "stage": "analysis", "title": "Each goal alone draws a different city", "text": "Solving for one measure at a time gave each a baseline and exposed the conflict. In Phase 1, clusters drawn on distance alone scored two to four times worse on vulnerability than the vulnerability-only clusters; clusters drawn on vulnerability alone cost 20–43% on distance. We scored every candidate as a ratio to each baseline (1.00 = as good as it gets) and swept the weights in tenths — GEO against SVI, then that blend against CON: 11 steps × 3 values of K × 2 models, 66 maps per sweep, 132 in all. We judged each on the ratios, population balance, the objective value and whether its clusters looked like whole pieces of city.", "layout": "gallery", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/austin/phase1-svi-only-k3.webp", "caption": "Phase 1, SVI only (K = 3): the vulnerable east side becomes one cluster. Each cluster is shaded by its population-weighted SVI.", "kind": "chart"}, {"src": "assets/projects/austin/phase1-geo-only-k3.webp", "caption": "GEO only (K = 3): compact clusters that each average out to the city's vulnerability — so the whole map turns one colour.", "kind": "chart"}, {"src": "assets/projects/austin/phase1-con-only-k3.webp", "caption": "CON only (K = 3): clusters drawn by travel between areas.", "kind": "chart"}]}, {"id": "quote", "stage": "recommendation", "title": "No clustering wins on every count", "text": "Even the most optimal solutions yield trade-offs between the parameters.", "layout": "quote", "media": []}, {"id": "recommendation", "stage": "recommendation", "title": "Two answers, and when to use each", "text": "Both phases ended at K = 2, but Phase 2 handed back two solutions instead of one. The all-pairs model (weights 0.4 GEO, 0.4 SVI, 0.2 CON) split Austin into two clusters within 1% of each other in population, 15% off the best on GEO and SVI and 10% on CON. The medoid model (0.54 / 0.36 / 0.10) won on its own objective but lost on every ratio — 1.19, 1.34 and 1.17 — and on population balance. Which one to use, we wrote, depends on the outbreak and what it makes planners care about most.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/austin/phase2-k2-two-models.webp", "caption": "Phase 2, K = 2. Left: all-pairs model (ratios 1.15 GEO, 1.15 SVI, 1.10 CON). Right: medoid model (1.19, 1.34, 1.17).", "kind": "chart"}]}, {"id": "next", "stage": "next", "title": "Check what \"15×\" and \"optimal\" meant", "text": "Two things I'd fix before trusting our own headline. The report says the all-pairs objective came out 15 times the medoid one — but all-pairs adds up hundreds of pair distances while the medoid model adds 44 distances to a centre, so the totals were never on the same scale. (In February I asked how the two objectives related; this is the answer I'd give now.) And we called the medoid solution \"mathematically optimal\" after runs capped at two minutes or a 5% gap; the gap belongs next to that claim. Then I'd test the 80% population floor, which we never varied.", "layout": "text", "media": []}]}, "bike-caliper": {"slug": "bike-caliper", "name": "Nylon Bike Caliper", "floor": "engineering", "onSite": true, "template": "engineering", "status": "approved", "oneLiner": "Nylon rear brake caliper arms, 47.7% lighter on the second print, and a lesson in leverage.", "context": {"course": "MECH_ENG 240 · Mechanical Design & Manufacturing", "when": "Spring 2026", "team": "Team of 6 (Group 18)", "partner": "", "role": "Mechanical designer — right-arm CAD & FEA optimization; documentation lead"}, "software": ["nx"], "skills": [{"name": "User needs → specs", "evidence": "needs"}, {"name": "Competitive analysis", "evidence": "research"}, {"name": "FEA & topology optimization", "evidence": "final"}, {"name": "CAD modelling", "evidence": "final"}, {"name": "Performance testing", "evidence": "prototype"}, {"name": "Technical writing", "evidence": "prototype"}, {"name": "Manufacturing planning", "evidence": "next"}], "classes": ["mecheng240"], "cover": "assets/projects/bike-caliper/final-pair-render.webp", "blocks": [{"id": "problem", "stage": "problem", "title": "Judged on stopping power and grams", "text": "The brief was two parts: a replacement pair of rear rim-brake caliper arms for a test bike, printed in Nylon 12 on a Formlabs Fuse 1+ SLS printer. They had to fit a fixed mount (an M6 pivot 47.5 mm above the pad hole, inside a 15 × 12 × 6 cm box), survive the braking load, and would be scored on stopping power and weight in two braking tests, in weeks 8 and 10.\n\nSix of us split the leads; I led documentation.", "layout": "text", "media": []}, {"id": "research", "stage": "research", "title": "Only two lengths were ours to choose", "text": "We benchmarked four commercial calipers (Shimano Tiagra BR-4700 at 193 g up to Shimano BR-R451 at 203 g) and took the test methods from ISO 4210-2 and 4210-4.\n\nThen the hand calculations: moment balances at the brake lever, the caliper and the wheel, plus conservation of energy, linking hand force to stopping distance. Of the ten variables in that equation, only two were ours: r_pad (pivot to pad) and r_cable (pivot to cable). Hand force rises with r_pad and falls with r_cable. We wrote that down in HW 2. It took two test days to believe it.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/bike-caliper/hand-calcs-lever-to-pad-force.webp", "caption": "Moment balance from lever to pad: the whole design lives in r_pad and r_cable.", "kind": "diagram"}]}, {"id": "needs", "stage": "needs", "title": "Nineteen needs, six numbers FEA could check", "text": "Our 19 needs became 19 metrics; these are the ones a simulation or a test day could check.\n! Metric | Ideal | Marginal | From\nMass of the pair | 193 g | 200 g | four benchmarked calipers\nStopping distance, dry, from 25 km/h | 12 m | 15 m | ISO 4210-2\nHand force at the lever | < 100 N | < 180 N | ISO 4210, grip-strength data\nFatigue life | 30,000 cycles | 20,000 cycles | about 10 brakings a mile\nAllowable stress | 21.9 MPa | | SLS Nylon-12 fatigue curve, safety factor 1.5\nCombined deflection of both arms | < 7.33 mm | | 11 mm geometry limit ÷ 1.5\nDesign loads: 219.6 N cable tension, 195.6 N pad friction.", "layout": "facts", "media": []}, {"id": "brainstorm", "stage": "brainstorm", "title": "Sketch the load path, then let TO argue", "text": "Before modelling, we sketched each arm with its forces marked: cable tension in, pivot reaction, pad friction out, so we could see the path the load had to take.\n\nThen topology optimization in NX. The tutorial setup used arbitrary forces and a lone pinned hole; we noted it should carry our real 219.6 N and 195.6 N loads instead. The result read clearly on both arms: the load runs along the outer edges, and the middle of each arm barely works. That is an I-beam or C-channel in disguise, so we hollowed the middles and kept material at the edges.", "layout": "gallery", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/bike-caliper/topology-optimization-right-arm.webp", "caption": "Topology optimization of the right arm: the material stays on the edges.", "kind": "cad"}]}, {"id": "prototype", "stage": "prototype", "title": "The first print stopped fast, pulled hard", "text": "Our first CAD arms were blocks. FEA put the right one at 81.67 MPa inside the pivot hole, far over our 21.9 MPa limit; curving its corners and cutting where topology optimization found little load brought it to 19.41 MPa.\n\nThe first SLS pair weighed 115.0 g. On test day it stopped in about 10.5 m, locking the wheel in 2 of 3 runs, but took 205 N at the lever against a 180 N limit. The pads were thicker than modelled and the cable had no path, so we drilled a hole through the right arm. My test notes became the next print's to-do list.", "layout": "gallery", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/bike-caliper/fea-right-arm-first-print-stress.webp", "caption": "NX stress plot of the first-print right arm (week 7). The hot spot is the pivot hole.", "kind": "screen"}, {"src": "assets/projects/bike-caliper/first-print-drilled-cable-hole.webp", "caption": "The first print after testing, with the cable hole we drilled on the day.", "kind": "prototype"}]}, {"id": "final", "stage": "final", "title": "Half the mass, almost the same squeeze", "text": "For the second print I reshaped the right arm, working from our test notes: thinner, clear of the real pads, the cable routed straight down. It got a gradual curve around the left arm and a cable hole clamped under the arm instead of a bolt, and went from 74.5 g to 36.3 g — about 70% of the pair's saving. The left arm dropped to 0.5 in thick, its cut-outs tuned over 8 quick FEA rounds.\n\nThe pair went from 115.0 g to 60.2 g (−47.7%). Combined deflection rose to 5.670 mm, under 7.33 mm. On the bike: 12.5 m and 200 N.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/bike-caliper/right-arm-first-print-vs-final-render.webp", "caption": "The right arm: first print (74.5 g) and my final design (36.3 g), same scale. Rendered from our STL files.", "kind": "render"}, {"src": "assets/projects/bike-caliper/final-pair-render.webp", "caption": "The final pair, 60.2 g together. Rendered from our STL files.", "kind": "render"}]}, {"id": "quote", "stage": "final", "title": "Stiffness was never the lever", "text": "Lighter arms flexed more, but the hand force barely moved: only the lever ratio could fix that.", "layout": "quote", "media": []}, {"id": "next", "stage": "next", "title": "Next time, move the cable, not material", "text": "Lever force only fell from 205 to 200 N, still over the 180 N limit, because hand force follows the ratio of r_pad to r_cable, not stiffness. The next arm should shorten r_pad or lengthen r_cable, then re-test until both land in the ideal range. Stress peaks of 23.4 and 22.5 MPa, just over 21.9, need bigger fillets; longer pad-bolt bosses would stop the pads tilting.\n\nIn aluminium, the route depends on who buys: die casting for 50,000 entry-level bikes a year, CNC machining for pricier bikes, printing for custom builds. Our materials search pointed to Al 8090-T851.", "layout": "text", "media": []}]}, "gamejam": {"slug": "gamejam", "name": "Northwestern Game Jam", "floor": "design", "onSite": true, "order": 0, "template": "design", "status": "approved", "oneLiner": "A horror game built in Unity: A college amateur exorcist hired to cleanse a haunted show home, only to uncover the unsettling story of its former resident.", "context": {"course": "", "when": "April–May 2026", "team": "Team of 4", "partner": "Northwestern Game Jam 2026 · theme “Twist of Fate”", "role": "Concept & narrative designer, Unity developer — pitch, design documents, in-game writing, gameplay scripting, 3D assets"}, "software": ["unity", "claude", "midjourney", "runway", "premiere", "tripo", "mixamo", "chatgpt"], "skills": [{"name": "Narrative design", "evidence": "premise"}, {"name": "Worldbuilding", "evidence": "build"}, {"name": "Branching narrative", "evidence": "final"}, {"name": "AI-assisted coding (Unity MCP)", "evidence": "logic"}, {"name": "3D modelling & animation", "evidence": "assets"}, {"name": "Version control & debugging", "evidence": "broke"}, {"name": "User testing", "evidence": "showcase"}], "classes": [], "cover": "assets/projects/gamejam.webp", "blocks": [{"id": "premise", "stage": "premise", "title": "A ghost story about who owns a house", "text": "Northwestern Game Jam 2026 gave teams five weeks, the theme Twist of Fate and a showcase on May 9. The four of us ended up with **Candell Handles**. A college student with a side hustle in Daoist rituals gets a call from a developer: a staged, move-in-ready house keeps scaring buyers away, and they will pay $5,000 to make it stop by Friday night.\n\nThe ghost is the last resident, who refused to sell and died there. The twist belongs to the exorcist: she thinks she was hired to solve a problem, and she is the last step of it.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/gamejam/title-screen.webp", "kind": "screen", "caption": "Title screen: Candell’s dorm desk, the talismans buried under pizza and soda cans."}]}, {"id": "research", "stage": "research", "title": "Rules borrowed from Parasite and Papers, Please", "text": "Our references were films and games. **Parasite** gave every version its question: the people living inside a house may have more claim to it than the person on the paperwork. **Papers, Please** gave the Airbnb version its verb: read a booking request, approve or deny it, live with the money. In the realtor version, house hunters were to chatter like the radio in Two Point Hospital, chipper and deadpan.\n", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/gamejam/paper-please.webp", "caption": "<Papers, Please>, the game we referenced, centers on reading and comparing documents to find clues and make decisions.", "kind": "photo"}]}, {"id": "build", "stage": "build", "title": "Learning Unity by building a neighbourhood", "text": "Unity was the steepest part of the jam. The project went onto Unity Version Control on April 21; two days later our first house model sat alone on an empty plane, and by April 27 we had a night street, a suburban block from an asset pack and placeholder mannequins walking it. The final game reuses a first-person horror template (its inventory, lockpicking and readable papers) and tells the story on paper: a newspaper, an obituary, seven letters. ", "layout": "gallery", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/gamejam/unity-first-house-import.webp", "caption": "April 23: our first house model, alone on an empty plane.", "kind": "screen"}, {"src": "assets/projects/gamejam/unity-night-street-blockout.webp", "caption": "April 26: a lit night street and a placeholder mannequin.", "kind": "screen"}, {"src": "assets/projects/gamejam/unity-neighborhood-aerial.webp", "caption": "April 27: the suburban block, from an asset pack.", "kind": "screen"}, {"src": "assets/projects/gamejam/unity-house-at-night.webp", "kind": "screen", "caption": "The house at night in the Unity editor; the camera preview shows the marble maze on the couch."}]}, {"id": "assets", "stage": "build", "title": "Props from a sentence, characters from Mixamo", "text": "Our props started as words. For the pie we typed a description into Tripo and got a model back; text prompts were usually enough. The letters and the newspaper took two steps: we wrote the copy first, had GPT turn it into images, then had Tripo build the objects from those. make this line better While the grandmother and the cat were ready to go models downloaded from sketchfab, the player Candel, were also built from scratch from midjourney to gpt to 3D model, then move with Mixamo animations. The trouble was always the same two things, too many faces and textures that didn't come out right. ", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/gamejam/newspaper-prop.webp", "caption": "The newspaper prop: the story is told on paper.", "kind": "render"}]}, {"id": "logic", "stage": "build", "title": "The rules underneath the scare", "text": "About 6,000 lines of our own C# sit on top of the horror template without changing it; new systems plug in through small bridges, so the template's doors, inventory and state machine keep working. \n\n! System | What it does\nGhost cat | Waits at the porch and leads the way: click her and she meows, fades and reappears at the next waypoint; the last time, she leaves a charm.\nCharms and pies | Every pickup is counted on screen. Enough pies unlock the pistol; the five charms are what the pool ritual needs.\nThe grandmother | Waits unseen in the hall closet until you open it, then hunts you. A trigger box around the house keeps her home: she chases past the door for four seconds, then is sent back to patrol. The axe only knocks her home; the pistol's one bullet ends her.\nTwo endings | The bullet ends her (Ending 1, told in crimson); five charms dropped into the pool release her (Ending 2, in amber).\nGuidance | One controller reads the inventory every frame and moves the objective and hint on screen.\nPuzzles | A two-phase marble maze, padlocked chests, a mailbox of bobby pins and letters.", "layout": "facts", "media": []}, {"id": "broke", "stage": "build", "title": "Vibecoding is debugging, debugging, and debugging", "text": "On May 7, two days before the showcase, Unity would only open our project in Safe Mode: 999+ compilation errors. The horror template had been imported twice, so every class in it existed twice (error CS0101).That night we fixed duplicate keys in the package lock and removed stray Windows thumbnail files from the project. After midnight the next sync brought hundreds of missing-prefab links. At 3:30 pm on May 8 the log finally read “All current bugs have been fixed”, and the last evening went to the opening and to putting the grandmother in.\n\nVibecoding made us faster and slower at once. The hardest part was communication: getting Claude to fix one thing without rewriting logic that already worked. And it only helped when the story was clear to us first. If we weren't sure what a scene was for, the AI wasn't either.", "layout": "gallery", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/gamejam/unity-safe-mode.webp", "caption": "May 7, 3:52 pm: the project would only open in Safe Mode.", "kind": "screen"}, {"src": "assets/projects/gamejam/unity-999-errors.webp", "caption": "999+ errors: the template's classes, defined twice (CS0101).", "kind": "screen"}, {"src": "assets/projects/gamejam/unity-missing-prefabs.webp", "caption": "After midnight: hundreds of missing-prefab links.", "kind": "screen"}, {"src": "assets/projects/gamejam/unity-version-control-fixes.webp", "caption": "The fix, commit by commit (e-mails blurred): my package-lock and thumbnail fixes on May 7, “All current bugs have been fixed” on May 8.", "kind": "screen"}]}, {"id": "final", "stage": "final", "title": "Player autonomy or linear storytelling?", "text": "You pick the lock, the grandmother appears, and a ghost cat leads you through the house: a flashlight, the letters, a marble maze on the couch, a basement chest whose code is the date on her last letter. Then she starts hunting you.\n\nDesigning the story was harder than building it. A horror game has to guide players, or they freeze in a dark hallway and stop, and our scope was too big to tell a great branching story. So it runs in a line: A leads to B leads to C leads to D. We gave players two ways through: collect pies for a pistol with one bullet and erase her, or collect charms and set her spirit free. Despite the underlying story we designed, most of the gameplay revolves around avoiding the grandmother, who chases the player whenever they enter her line of sight. This works well as a horror mechanic, but the hidden story elements could be communicated more clearly.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/gamejam/weixin-image-20260929000145-40-24.webp", "caption": "The grandmother suddenly jumps out of a box in the basement, startling the player with a jumpscare.", "kind": "photo"}]}, {"id": "showcase", "stage": "next", "title": "Demo Day!", "text": "On May 9 the showcase ran all day in the SAI SoundTank at the Frances Searle Building: demos and judging in the morning, a panel, then free play until the awards. Each team had a table, and someone had to present to qualify. Four judges played: two game-design instructors, one from Northwestern and one from UChicago; the head of Night City, Chicago's indie game community space; and a sound designer for games. Our hint text wasn't enough on the floor. Being chased is distracting, so we stood next to players and talked them through what to do.\n\nThis is the first time I ever built a video game from scratch. Unity was our biggest lesson: a shared project breaks in ways a single file never does, through a doubled import, a lock file, two operating systems, and fixing it is part of making the game. I also took away how game logic holds a story together, how to tell a story well, and how much autonomy to leave to the player.", "layout": "text", "media": []}]}, "levelup": {"slug": "levelup", "name": "LevelUp", "floor": "engineering", "onSite": true, "order": 0, "template": "engineering", "status": "approved", "oneLiner": "A clip-on bubble level for barbells, built to improve your gym performances with leveled exercises.", "context": {"course": "DSGN 308 · Human-Centered Product Design", "when": "Spring 2025", "team": "Team of 7", "partner": "Segal Design Institute", "role": "Design researcher & CAD — problem finding, concept sketching, clip modelling, business model"}, "software": ["slides", "solidworks"], "skills": [{"name": "Observational research (AEIOU)", "evidence": "problem"}, {"name": "User interviews", "evidence": "problem"}, {"name": "Competitive analysis", "evidence": "problem"}, {"name": "Design criteria & decision matrices", "evidence": "problem"}, {"name": "Brainwriting", "evidence": "brainstorm"}, {"name": "Sketching", "evidence": "brainstorm"}, {"name": "Business model", "evidence": "next"}, {"name": "3D printing (FDM)", "evidence": "final"}, {"name": "CAD modelling", "evidence": "final"}], "classes": ["dsgn308"], "cover": "assets/projects/levelup.webp", "blocks": [{"id": "problem", "stage": "problem", "title": "Grocery bags or barbell?", "text": "DSGN 308 makes us identify a personal problem in our daily life expriences and solve it by developing a physical product. Our seven-person team pooled a 55-response survey, AEIOU sessions and our own complaints into 122 opportunities, kept the 44 a product could fix, and chased three: a messy machine shop, slipping grocery-bag straps, and the gym.\n\nWhile I led the grocery space, I tested our initial assumption through a 20-person survey, 10 user interviews, and a competitive analysis of eight existing solutions. The data weakened our original hypothesis: slipping straps ranked sixth out of seven grocery frustrations, averaged only 2.8/5 in severity, and was never independently mentioned by any of our interviewees. Meanwhile, the gym team combined observations, interviews, and timed trials: they saw 3 of 5 bench press users lifting unevenly within a 20-minute observation and measured an average 4.5-minute wait for occupied cardio machines. After comparing the two gym problems across five criteria, uneven barbell lifting narrowly won, and our final weighted matrix—impact, feasibility, team interest, novelty, and market size—put the gym first at 82.5, ahead of the shop at 70.5 and groceries at 63, which prompted us to eventually move forward with the gym space opportunity.", "layout": "gallery", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/levelup/grocery.webp", "caption": "", "kind": "photo"}]}, {"id": "research", "stage": "research", "title": "Commonly overlooked", "text": "Two teammates spent an hour at SPAC, the campus gym: 30 minutes of AEIOU observation, then five interviews. Barbells were the busiest equipment, spotters were rare, and in 20 minutes three people lifted a visibly uneven bar. Most interviewees didn't see it as their problem, including one we had just watched do it.\n\nA Northwestern lift coach said mild upper-body imbalance is usually fine, but in extreme cases a raised shoulder can lead to impingement, rotator-cuff trouble and, at worst, a torn labrum. The fixes on offer ran from a $274.99 barbell with a built-in level to a $3,999 smart gym, or another person watching you.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/levelup/benchmark-existing-fixes.webp", "caption": "Our benchmark of seven existing fixes, +1/0/−1 on cost, access, effectiveness, convenience and safety. Most cost money or need another person; dumbbells only help up to a point.", "kind": "chart"}]}, {"id": "quote", "stage": "research", "title": "Everyone sees it — in someone else", "text": "“I don't personally have problems with it because I have good body control. I notice it in other people though.” — a gym-goer at SPAC, whom we had just watched bench unevenly", "layout": "quote", "media": []}, {"id": "needs", "stage": "needs", "title": "Every need needs a number!", "text": "Our first spec sheet asked for “fits in pocket” and “universally understandable”; by the final report every need had a number. The final prototype, tested with three lifters at SPAC on June 10:\n\n! Need | Spec | LevelUp\nVisible | Testers rate how well they saw it: ≥ 4/5 | 4.35/5\nIntuitive | On the bar without instructions in < 5 s | 2.5 s average\nUnobstructive | Never touches the lifter | Hit 1 of 3 chests: miss\nPortable | Fits an average gym bag | 2.76 × 1.34 × 1.21 in\nAdaptable | 2+ exercises | 4 (we simulated them): bench, spotted squat, row, curl\nAdaptable | Fits 28.5 mm bars ± 3 mm | 28.5 mm only: miss\nCost-effective | Unit cost < $10 | $1.30 or $3.90 (our figures differ): pass\nBalance-effective | ≥ 90% of reps level | 100% (60% with the vial hidden)\nIntentional | Each rep slows by > 1 s | 0.98 s: a miss the report rounded up", "layout": "facts", "media": []}, {"id": "brainstorm", "stage": "brainstorm", "title": "The obvious idea showed up six times", "text": "We brainwrote alone first, ten ideas each, so no single voice set the direction: 70 ideas, then three sketches apiece, 21 in all. Lasers, accelerometers, phone apps and wearables all appeared, but six of our seven lists had a bubble level on them.\n\nMine had three, plus three ideas for marking where your hands go. I sketched a level vial capped with magnets, to stick straight onto a steel bar; magnets turned up on two other lists too, and the sketch made our final deck. We took three directions into mockups: a plain level, a phone running a level app, and a bearing that slides when the bar tips.", "layout": "gallery", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/levelup/sketch-magnetic-level-kay.webp", "caption": "My sketch for the May 11 concept round: a level vial capped with magnets.", "kind": "sketch"}, {"src": "assets/projects/levelup/sketch-phone-on-bar.webp", "caption": "A teammate's sketch: the phone as the level, clipped to the bar.", "kind": "sketch"}, {"src": "assets/projects/levelup/sketch-bearing-clips.webp", "caption": "A teammate's sketch: bearing cuffs that slide into your hand when the bar tips.", "kind": "sketch"}]}, {"id": "prototype", "stage": "prototype", "title": "Three mockups ", "text": "We built cheap: cardboard, velcro, zip ties and a $5.99 five-pack of magnetic levels. The first two rounds (May 21–22) only tested how a level holds on. Magnets won; the shaft collars took too long, the velcro nearly hit a chin, and the magnetic tape we ordered wouldn't stick to the gym's bars.\n\nThen we pitted three directions against each other. The lift coach preferred the bearing, our class the phone, gym-goers the plain level. We chose the level on our requirements: most intuitive, portable, cheapest and buildable in the time left. Its flaw came with it: a small bubble that rolls out of view as the bar turns.", "layout": "gallery", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/levelup/mockup-r1-velcro.webp", "caption": "Round 1 (May 21), velcro on cardboard: easy on, but the straps nearly hit a tester's chin.", "kind": "mockup"}, {"src": "assets/projects/levelup/mockup-r2-c-clip.webp", "caption": "Round 2's 1-inch clip was the fastest on, but too big for the gym's bars, so it spun. The clip came back at the end.", "kind": "mockup"}, {"src": "assets/projects/levelup/mockup-phone-stand.webp", "caption": "The phone stand: the most responsive readout, until its magnets let go and the phone fell as a tester lowered the bar.", "kind": "mockup"}, {"src": "assets/projects/levelup/test-bubble-vs-bearing.webp", "caption": "Bubble (left) against bearing (right): the bearing only started sliding after 0.7–2.7° of tilt, the bubble at once.", "kind": "test"}, {"src": "assets/projects/levelup/test-split-bubble.webp", "caption": "Lift-coach session: the bar met the coach's chest and the bubble split into several. Which one is level?", "kind": "test"}, {"src": "assets/projects/levelup/prototype-v1-two-levels.webp", "caption": "Final concept v1 (June 10): two levels on fabric, so one is always in view. The fabric let them twist out of line, and testers watched only one.", "kind": "prototype"}]}, {"id": "final", "stage": "final", "title": "The losing mockup gave the winner its angle", "text": "We rebuilt the level from scratch; I helped model the clip in CAD and print it. LevelUp is a printed black PLA clip that snaps over the bar, with no straps or magnets, and a 1-inch vial press-fit on an arm standing just under an inch off the bar. That angle came from the phone stand we had dropped: testers kept tilting the phone forward to face them.\n\nOn June 10, three SPAC lifters clipped it on without instructions in 2.5 seconds on average. With the vial hidden, the bubble left the level zone nine times over their reps; in view, never. It also hit one tester's chest and fits only one bar size.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/levelup/levelup-product.webp", "caption": "LevelUp: a compliant PLA clip, and a 1-inch vial press-fit on a 90° arm.", "kind": "prototype"}, {"src": "assets/projects/levelup/levelup-lifter-view.webp", "caption": "What the lifter sees during a barbell row: the vial between the hands.", "kind": "test"}]}, {"id": "next", "stage": "next", "title": "Million Dollar Vision", "text": "I built our business model canvas: beginner lifters buying direct; rec centres, gyms and trainers buying wholesale; a keychain level as a side line (my first brainstorm idea, back again). We priced it at $9.99 retail, $7.99 wholesale. Reading the canvas now, it still pitches a “magnetic clip-on”, and our cost table prices a magnet-and-rubber build at $3.90 a unit. The part we tested was printed plastic and a vial. Next time I'd cost the object on the bench.\n\nFor future iterations, we would choose a clip for 25.5–31.5 mm bars, a shape that clears the chest, rubber for grip, and a thicker fluid for experienced lifters.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/levelup/business-model-canvas.webp", "caption": "My business model canvas for LevelUp, June 2025.", "kind": "diagram"}]}]}, "notipclip": {"slug": "notipclip", "name": "No-Tip-Clip", "floor": "engineering", "onSite": true, "order": 1, "template": "engineering", "status": "approved", "oneLiner": "A 3D-printed clamp and caster that works like a training wheel for back-heavy halo-traction wheelchairs.", "context": {"course": "DSGN 106-2 · Design Thinking & Communication 2", "when": "Spring 2024", "team": "Team of 4", "partner": "Shriners Children's Chicago", "role": "User researcher & technical writer — interviews, mockup testing, SolidWorks modelling, documentation"}, "software": ["solidworks", "cura"], "skills": [{"name": "User interviews", "evidence": "research"}, {"name": "Client communication", "evidence": "research"}, {"name": "Sketching", "evidence": "brainstorm"}, {"name": "Low-fi mockups", "evidence": "prototype"}, {"name": "Proxy-user testing", "evidence": "prototype"}, {"name": "Technical writing", "evidence": "final"}, {"name": "Risk assessment", "evidence": "next"}, {"name": "CAD modelling", "evidence": "final"}, {"name": "3D printing (FDM)", "evidence": "final"}], "classes": ["dsgn106-2"], "cover": "assets/projects/notipclip.webp", "blocks": [{"id": "problem", "stage": "problem", "title": "When the chair tips, the pull goes sideways", "text": "Shriners Children's Chicago prepares children with severe scoliosis and kyphosis for spinal fusion with halo traction: a ring pinned to the skull, a rope over a boom on the back of a walker or wheelchair, and weights that slowly stretch the spine. The boom makes the wheelchair back-heavy, and a child is often too light to hold it down. When the chair tips, the weights sway and pull diagonally instead of straight up. Our partner, a clinician there, listed six problems with the equipment. We took this one, with ten weeks and a $150 class budget.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/notipclip/traction-wheelchair-back.webp", "caption": "A traction wheelchair at Shriners Children's Chicago, from behind: the boom rises from the backrest and the traction rope runs down the back. Site visit, April 2024.", "kind": "photo"}]}, {"id": "research", "stage": "research", "title": "The current fix: sandbags that slip", "text": "We started with secondary research on halo traction. I set up our partner interview and site visit by email, wrote the interview guide, and was its lead interviewer on the Zoom call. On April 11 three of us visited the hospital; no patients were there, so we measured, photographed and handled the equipment. The hospital's fix for tipping is weight: cuff weights and sandbags velcroed or taped to the frame, 8–10 lb for a child. They slip, and they get in the way of the front wheels. We also learned that the clamps holding the traction frame on are no longer made.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/notipclip/cuff-weight-fix.webp", "caption": "The hospital's fix: a cuff weight strapped to the frame with Velcro.", "kind": "photo"}, {"src": "assets/projects/notipclip/worn-circular-clamp.webp", "caption": "A worn circular clamp, measured on site. Its supplier no longer makes it.", "kind": "photo"}]}, {"id": "needs", "stage": "needs", "title": "From improving everything to one clamp", "text": "Our first mission statement promised a safer, more durable device overall. Three revisions later it named one part: a modular clamp with an adjustable nut-and-bolt height. Every spec but the budget came from our partner.\n\n!Need | Spec\nStops tipping | The wheelchair does not tip\nDetachable | Clips on and off\nQuick | Under 5 minutes to set up\nLight | Under 3 lb\nDurable | Takes rough use without losing effect\nAffordable | Under $150 (class budget)\n\nWe would judge the mockups on size, weight, safety, effectiveness and modularity.", "layout": "facts", "media": []}, {"id": "brainstorm", "stage": "brainstorm", "title": "Our first instinct: fight weight with weight", "text": "Each of us took three needs and sketched ten ideas for each. My thirty ran from a rubber lining for the slipping clamps to gym-style pin holes for height, plus ten answers to the weight imbalance, seven of them adding or moving weight. We pooled our lists, ranked our top fives in private and agreed on eight ideas. Our first matrix compared only weight concepts; the professors asked for broader options, checked against duration, stability and feasibility. I then drew mockup plans for three concepts: a training wheel, an adjustable weight bag and a weight-balance calculator.", "layout": "gallery", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/notipclip/kay-ideas-weight.webp", "caption": "My ten ideas for the weight imbalance. Seven add or move weight.", "kind": "sketch"}, {"src": "assets/projects/notipclip/kay-ideas-clamp.webp", "caption": "My ten ideas for clamps that wear out and slide, starting with a rubber friction lining.", "kind": "sketch"}, {"src": "assets/projects/notipclip/mockup-plan-training-wheel.webp", "caption": "Mockup plan, April 25: three places a training wheel could attach.", "kind": "sketch"}, {"src": "assets/projects/notipclip/mockup-plan-weight-bag.webp", "caption": "Mockup plan: where adjustable weights could sit under the seat.", "kind": "sketch"}]}, {"id": "prototype", "stage": "prototype", "title": "Past five kilos, the bag stopped helping", "text": "In May we tested two mockups in the Segal Prototyping Lab and outdoors, as proxy users; the calculator never left paper. I helped rig the weight bag under the chair, against a 7 kg hanging weight, and wrote the report.\n\n!Weight bag | 1 kg | 3 kg | 5 kg | 10 kg\nStability | 2/10 | 5/10 | 8/10 | 8/10\nFlexibility | 5/10 | 7/10 | 6/10 | 4/10\n\nPast 5 kg, extra weight bought no stability and cost flexibility, and the bag was hard to place the same way twice. The wheel steadied the chair and promised one size for all. We decided to move forward with that.", "layout": "facts", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/notipclip/training-wheel-mockup.webp", "caption": "The training-wheel mockup held to the back of a wheelchair. Its row of holes set the height, and left thin metal between them.", "kind": "mockup"}]}, {"id": "quote", "stage": "", "title": "", "text": "Instead of adding weight, we added a point of contact with the floor.", "layout": "quote", "media": []}, {"id": "final", "stage": "final", "title": "A $62 training wheel that clamps on", "text": "The No-Tip-Clip is a two-piece clamp that I helped model in SolidWorks, printed at 95% infill in about 50 hours. It bolts around the wheelchair's lowest rear rod; an oval hole about 1.5 times the radius of standard frame tubing lets one clamp grip different frames. A ⅞-inch bolt through its side carries a caster: turn it until the wheel just touches the floor, then lock two nuts. Although we planned to weld the bolt to the caster, for technical limitations, we used a band-sawn polycarbonate plate to join them instead. The costs for those parts came with a total of $62.26, which was well within our $150 budget.", "layout": "gallery", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/notipclip/cad-render.webp", "caption": "The final design in CAD: printed clamp, ⅞-inch bolt, caster.", "kind": "cad"}, {"src": "assets/projects/notipclip/installed.webp", "caption": "The prototype on the lowest rear rod of a wheelchair, wheel just touching the floor.", "kind": "prototype"}, {"src": "assets/projects/notipclip/setting-height.webp", "caption": "Setting the height: turn the wheel on the bolt, then lock the nuts. From the instructions for use.", "kind": "prototype"}]}, {"id": "next", "stage": "next", "title": "Next version: no tools, no tilt", "text": "Our closing slide ticked three of four requirements; fitting different equipment stayed unticked. On a sloped rod the wheel leans, so angle and attachment top our next steps, with Velcro clips, an improved wheel, and metal or a weld in place of the plastic plate. The safety evaluation we drafted ranked vibration through the clamp as the top risk, so add damping, plus the load test we promised at the design review. ", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/notipclip/sloped-rod-tilt.webp", "caption": "On a sloped rod the wheel leans. The angle is the next problem to solve.", "kind": "prototype"}]}]}, "plu": {"slug": "plu", "name": "People Like Us", "floor": "design", "onSite": true, "order": 2, "template": "design", "status": "approved", "oneLiner": "Brand system for a Copenhagen brewery where most staff are neurodivergent: one dot, same area, any shape.", "context": {"course": "DIS Copenhagen · Graphic Design Foundations Studio", "when": "Summer 2026 (presented July 31)", "team": "Solo studio project", "partner": "People Like Us (PLU), Copenhagen — the studio’s client", "role": "Brand designer — research, identity system, packaging, posters; web tool built with Claude Code"}, "software": ["indesign", "illustrator", "photoshop", "aftereffects", "claude", "html", "javascript", "github"], "skills": [{"name": "Secondary research", "evidence": "research"}, {"name": "Sketching", "evidence": "concept"}, {"name": "Concept selection", "evidence": "concept"}, {"name": "Worldbuilding", "evidence": "iterate"}, {"name": "Visual identity", "evidence": "final"}, {"name": "Posters & presentations", "evidence": "final"}], "classes": ["dis2026"], "cover": "assets/projects/plu/can-mockup-three-characters.webp", "blocks": [{"id": "premise", "stage": "premise", "title": "A bar where nobody has to hide", "text": "People Like Us (PLU) is a Copenhagen brewery with a hashtag for a mission, #SOCIALREVOLUTIONBYBEER: a real workplace where neurodivergent people can work without hiding who they are. 70% of its staff are neurodivergent, and they develop the beer, can it and pour it. Its next step is Denmark’s first non-alcoholic bar and microbrewery, and in summer 2026 my DIS graphic design studio designed for it.\n\nI framed the goals as two: meaningful jobs for neurodivergent people, and public awareness of neurodivergence. My answer was a design rule: an interactive system that builds understanding by making the idea personal and fun to take part in.", "layout": "full", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/plu/plot-lulu-105cm.webp", "kind": "render", "caption": "My 105 × 105 cm plot, the studio’s final format: Lulu, the three cans, colour, type and the flow on one sheet."}]}, {"id": "research", "stage": "research", "title": "Small breweries can afford strange cans", "text": "The studio began with a beer tasting and a meeting with PLU; the instructor’s brief set the angles. My slides started from what PLU already had: a pink bar, a bold logo, playful beer names. It works like a phantom brewery, brewing its recipes with other microbreweries, and wants the bar to be a hub for music, quizzes, knitting circles and poetry, more than a pub.\n\nSorting cans into interesting and not interesting to look at settled one thing: the dull ones were the big brands, and small breweries can afford strong names and odd illustrations. PLU lacked one identity across cans, posters, menus, merch and the room.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/plu/research-cans-interesting-vs-not.webp", "kind": "diagram", "caption": "From my research slides: cans I found interesting to look at, and cans I didn’t."}]}, {"id": "concept", "stage": "concept", "title": "Everyone gets the same pour", "text": "The class agreed on three flavours: a fruity cider, a wheaty amber ale and a dark chocolate porter. My first concept gave each one a character from Nordic myth who was misjudged or mistreated in their story: Ratatoskr, Þjálfi and Ótr, drawn in black ink in week three.\n\nThe idea that lasted came from the tasting itself. In a flight, every glass holds the same pour: people like you + people like me = people like us. So the brand became one shape that always keeps the same area and changes shape freely, in loud colour. Everyone gets the same amount; the difference is the shape.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/plu/first-direction-nordic-characters.webp", "kind": "sketch", "caption": "Week three: Ratatoskr, Þjálfi and Ótr, one misjudged character per flavour."}, {"src": "assets/projects/plu/tasting-flight-poster.webp", "kind": "sketch", "caption": "July 13: the characters in a tasting flight. Same glass, same pour."}]}, {"id": "rule", "stage": "concept", "title": "The one thing the system refuses", "text": "You can stretch it, spike it, throw it off balance — the one thing the system refuses to let you do is make yourself smaller.", "layout": "quote", "media": []}, {"id": "build", "stage": "build", "title": "Three variables and one rule", "text": "I built the dot maker by directing Claude, not by typing the code: 19 conversations, 10 coding sessions, over 350 prompts, then 40 commits from July 14 to 30. Claude wrote the code; my prompts decided what each feature is and the rule it can't break.\n\nThe first prompt was the rule itself: the dots \"should fit in a box and default as a circle. You can pinch the shape but the area stays the same — one part goes in, another comes out.\" Shape, colour and how sharp the lines are: \"these three variables are easy to write\", so instead of drawing the dots myself I asked for an interface where visitors pinch their own.\n\nUnder it, a dot is 96 radii around a centre. A drag pulls the radii near your finger toward it, smooths them, then rescales all of them until the area is the circle's again, which is why the badge can say \"Area = 100% · always\". On July 14 I asked Claude to put it on GitHub \"so it opens in a browser as a small app — mobile display is the priority.\"", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/plu/dot-maker-first-online-jul14.webp", "kind": "screen", "caption": "The first version online (July 14), re-run from the repo: \"Push it in somewhere, it comes out somewhere else — the area never changes.\""}]}, {"id": "dress", "stage": "build", "title": "Dressing without changing the person", "text": "Every feature after that came as a prompt with its rule attached.\n\n- **Eyes**: left, right or centre, with sliders for size, gap and height; then \"Cyclops and Googly are the only two I need.\"\n- **Clothes**: an ID-photo torso, about a third of the chest, whose shape and position never change; outfits are only patterns inside it. Their colours are muted sorbets so they never upstage the neon face, because the face is the person: \"the torso and clothes are external dressing.\"\n- **Hair** (July 19): ten girl and ten boy hairstyles as abstract geometric shapes. A hair colour too close to the face colour is greyed out, so hair never melts into a face.\n- **Hats** (July 22): hair or a hat, never both, including the chef's toque Carl wears, plus seven more outfits.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/plu/dot-maker-demo.mp4", "kind": "screen", "caption": "My screen recording of the dot maker: pinch the dot (the area stays 100%), add eyes, try outfits, hair and hats."}]}, {"id": "hair", "stage": "build", "title": "When the face skews, the hair floats", "text": "Pinch a face hard enough and the first hairstyles floated: they were drawn for a circle, so a stretched head poked out of the hair. I asked for \"a few approaches\" to guarantee the hair covers the top of the head, and chose two to try first. Twenty minutes after hair went in, the fix: the fringe is the face's own deformed outline, scaled up about 7% and clipped to a band at the top, so it covers the scalp by construction, and buns, tails and puffs attach to the real edge of the face.\n\nHalf an hour later, the eyes: \"Half-covered is actually kind of cute, but fully covered obviously can't happen.\" Now a fringe can cut no lower than the eyes' centre line.", "layout": "gallery", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/plu/hair-fix-before-after.webp", "kind": "screen", "caption": "One pinched face, rendered by the app at each commit on July 19: 16:54, the head pokes out of the hair; 17:16, the fringe follows the face. With the eyes set high: 17:16, the fringe hides them; 17:51, it stops at their centre line."}]}, {"id": "match", "stage": "build", "title": "People like me + people like you", "text": "The brand's formula became a feature. After you join the pool, \"Find people like me\" looks for the dot most like yours and draws a card: your name + theirs = People Like Us, and how alike you are, \"— the rest is the interesting part.\" The score is 70% shape, 15% face colour and 15% eyes, clothes and hair. Shape is compared at all 96 rotations of the outline, so what counts is how you're pinched, not which way you lean. If the pool is empty: \"You're the first one here. Someone like you will show up.\"", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/plu/find-people-like-me-card.webp", "kind": "screen", "caption": "A card from the app, made for this page with two test dots named after my characters."}]}, {"id": "live", "stage": "build", "title": "From a web page to a bar", "text": "Then it had to live in a bar. I asked for a white page with a kiosk in the middle, \"very realistic\" and not flat, with every display and button still visible, and one condition: \"Always make sure the dot maker's functionality is not impaired.\" A button on the kiosk opens the Cleveland Clinic's page on neurodivergence, so the machine also explains what the bar is about.\n\nNext, the loop the whole system rests on: people \"scan a QR code to make their own person, put it in the pool, see themselves on the big screen (metro ad). There won't be a real metro ad… but I still want the real-time update mechanism. What is the easiest way to achieve that?\" The answer was Firestore, a cloud database: every phone adds its dot to one shared list and the screen listens to it. Anyone can read the list and add to it; no one can delete from it. Without a connection, the app syncs between browser tabs on one computer instead. On July 27 every phone's pool joined the same list; dots have no IDs, so the app tells them apart by their shape, colour, clothes and name.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/plu/dot-maker-face-and-dress.webp", "kind": "screen", "caption": "The dot maker inside the kiosk: every display and button still works."}, {"src": "assets/projects/plu/big-screen-first-live-wall-jul24.webp", "kind": "screen", "caption": "The first live wall (July 24, 00:37), re-run with test dots: everyone in the pool, and a QR to join."}]}, {"id": "screens", "stage": "iterate", "title": "One night, eight screens", "text": "Making the screen look like PLU took one night. Between 00:37 and 02:23 on July 24 I went through eight looks: a grid of everyone; a drifting crowd that parts for each newcomer, with the formula in the middle; a portrait poster in the six can colours, reverted ten minutes later; a two-colour silhouette field; faces and personal colours back, in American Typewriter; dark, heads only; acid-green rules with film grain, scanlines and a vignette; a condensed display face with glitches.\n\nIn my presentation this is the \"visual coherence\" step, and I quoted myself: \"This is too ugly, I'll do it myself.\" By noon the screen was my poster, drawn in Illustrator, and the app only filled its boxes with the live parts: a see-through kiosk, the QR and the feed of new dots. That afternoon I had it export any dot in the pool as a transparent PNG, so the characters could leave the app for my own layouts, the cans included. On July 30, the night before the presentation, the poster half went and the screen became one full-bleed live feed.", "layout": "gallery", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/plu/big-screen-one-night.webp", "kind": "screen", "caption": "The big screen at each version, re-run from the repo with test dots: July 24 from 01:04 to 02:23, my Illustrator poster at 12:29, and the live feed of July 30."}]}, {"id": "logo", "stage": "build", "title": "I want the collision", "text": "The logo is two eight-lobed splats spinning against each other, and the kiosk shows it animated. Rebuilding that animation in code, I had to choose between two designs, and I said so: \"The deep hug and the lobe-on-lobe collision are two different designs. I want the collision.\"\n\nBlending the shapes where they touch (a smooth minimum) couldn't give me that: a blend strong enough to join them where they meet also joins the neighbouring lobes, which leaves only a few pixels of settings that work, and I pointed that out before we tuned it. Pushing them apart after each hit did work, but 45 px of recoil made the whole logo throb and competed with the squash: \"I want the squash to be the visible thing.\" Before it rendered 48 frames, I asked for the clearance at each step from t = 0 to 5, to check the gap first.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/plu/logo-rebuild-impact-frame.webp", "kind": "render", "caption": "An impact frame: the lobes squash against each other instead of melting into one blob."}]}, {"id": "terminal", "stage": "build", "title": "A design terminal, to talk to Claude in pictures", "text": "By the last week, describing colours and outfits in words had become the slow part. So I asked for \"my own design terminal, just for myself, so it's easier to communicate to you visually\": a hidden panel on the app where I add, delete and retune face colours, hair colours and outfits and see them live. My edits stay in my browser; the panel turns them into a spec, a change list I hand back to Claude to apply to the real app.\n\nThe first version could manage outfits but not draw one: \"The terminal doesn't let me design the outfit like a brush.\" Twenty-six minutes later it had an Outfit Lab, where I paint a fit with dots and stripes I can drag; saved, it appears in the outfit picker.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/plu/design-terminal-outfit-lab.webp", "kind": "screen", "caption": "The design terminal beside the kiosk: the Outfit Lab with a test fit, notes, and the spec for Claude."}]}, {"id": "awareness", "stage": "build", "title": "Awareness in small moments", "text": "My second goal was public awareness of neurodivergence. On July 30, the night before the presentation, it went into the app as small moments:\n\n! Where | What\nAfter you join | “Your dot is 100% — same as everyone's. Just shaped differently.”\nSaved PNG | A strip with the slogan “there's always a spot for people like us”\nBig screen | 200 short facts about neurodivergence, one at a time, only while nobody is joining (a new dot always wins the screen); every seventh is the house slogan\nThe eyes | Rare blinks and sideways glances on the live dot, off for people who turn off motion; saved dots keep their eyes open\nRecording | A clean mode that hides the on-screen text, for filming the screen", "layout": "facts", "media": []}, {"id": "iterate", "stage": "iterate", "title": "The birth of Carl, Lulu, and Astrid.", "text": "The cans took four rounds in ten days, where I built character from the tool I built and then use them to design cans. On July 20 his label told a story in a paragraph (his bakery opens at six, he sells one kind of bread) and ended in a line you finish yourself: “People like us: like Carl and…”, beside an empty frame for your sticker. Two days later, a wheat pattern. By July 24 he stood behind his bread and the words were down to a name, a job and a flavour. The final label dropped the nutrition panel too.", "layout": "gallery", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/plu/carl-label-1-story-jul20.webp", "kind": "render", "caption": "July 20: a story, and a frame for your sticker."}, {"src": "assets/projects/plu/carl-label-2-wheat-jul22.webp", "kind": "render", "caption": "July 22: the wheat pattern."}, {"src": "assets/projects/plu/carl-label-3-bakery-jul24.webp", "kind": "render", "caption": "July 24: Carl at work; name, job, flavour."}, {"src": "assets/projects/plu/carl-label-4-final-jul30.webp", "kind": "render", "caption": "July 30: the print file."}]}, {"id": "final", "stage": "final", "title": "Your dot ends up on the can", "text": "I presented the system on July 31. A poster asks you to scan a QR code and join Social Revolution By Beer. On your phone you pinch your dot, dress it and join the pool. A big screen shows you arrive in real time through a Firestore database, and a printer at the bar turns your dot into a sticker for the empty frame on your can. The screen (a metro ad) and the printer exist only in my mock-ups.\n\nThe cans carry Lulu the cashier, Carl the baker and Astrid the barista, whom I made with the same tool guests use. A tote and a T-shirt carry them too.", "layout": "gallery", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/plu/flow-kiosk-phone-printer.webp", "kind": "mockup", "caption": "The flow, in mock-ups: the metro-ad screen, the dot maker at the bar, the sticker printer."}, {"src": "assets/projects/plu/poster-social-revolution-by-beer.webp", "kind": "render", "caption": "The poster: QR code, a pile of dots, Social Revolution By Beer."}, {"src": "assets/projects/plu/tote-9am-9pm-mockup.webp", "kind": "mockup", "caption": "Tote: 9 AM coffee, 9 PM beer, same bar."}]}, {"id": "next", "stage": "next", "title": "", "text": "", "layout": "text", "media": []}]}, "slidemaster": {"slug": "slidemaster", "name": "Slide Master", "floor": "engineering", "onSite": true, "order": 2, "template": "engineering", "status": "approved", "oneLiner": "A wheelchair-to-shower transfer board that uses sliding mechanism to prevent skin injury in patients.", "context": {"course": "DSGN 106-1 · Design Thinking & Communication 1", "when": "Winter 2024", "team": "Team of 4", "partner": "Occupational therapist, spinal-cord-injury rehabilitation", "role": "Mockup & documentation lead — mockup plans, CAD and drawings, design freeze, concept rationale"}, "software": ["tinkercad", "onshape"], "skills": [{"name": "Competitive analysis", "evidence": "research"}, {"name": "User interviews", "evidence": "research"}, {"name": "User needs → specs", "evidence": "needs"}, {"name": "Sketching", "evidence": "brainstorm"}, {"name": "Low-fi mockups", "evidence": "brainstorm"}, {"name": "Engineering drawings", "evidence": "prototype"}, {"name": "Posters & presentations", "evidence": "final"}, {"name": "Technical writing", "evidence": "final"}, {"name": "CAD modelling", "evidence": "prototype"}], "classes": ["dsgn106-1"], "cover": "assets/projects/slidemaster.webp", "blocks": [{"id": "problem", "stage": "problem", "title": "Damage the user can't feel", "text": "An occupational therapist who helps people with spinal-cord injuries manage daily tasks like bathing brought our DTC team a problem. Her patients cross from wheelchair to shower chair on a transfer board, usually a flat plank with two hand holes, scooting across it on bare, often wet skin they cannot feel. So friction damage goes unnoticed. Boards also slip off the tub ledge, are hard to get onto, and pinch fingers. A patient who can't transfer safely is left with bed baths. Since the boards are paid for by insurance, we were asked to keep our budget around $60.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/slidemaster/scenario-storyboard.webp", "caption": "Scenario breakdown from our first research deck: wheelchair beside the tub, board across the gap, patient on the shower chair.", "kind": "sketch"}]}, {"id": "research", "stage": "research", "title": "The obvious fix already existed", "text": "We began with what's on the market: the plain wooden board, a tub transfer bench, the ShowerGlyde sliding system, ceiling lifts and the $249 BeasyGlyder. Our partner filled in the rest on a video call, where I was one of the note-takers. The surprise came on our week-5 visit to her unit. Sliding boards already existed, and staff rarely used them: they add instability, and their seats are narrower than the board, so skin hangs over the edge, drags and gets caught in the mechanism. Nobody there liked curved boards either. We weren't short of ideas, yet each existing one failed somewhere specific.", "layout": "text", "media": []}, {"id": "research-quote", "stage": "research", "title": "The rule we'd end up breaking", "text": "“Less material you have to get under your butt, the easier.” — our partner", "layout": "quote", "media": []}, {"id": "needs", "stage": "needs", "title": "Quantified Needs", "text": "Our first project definition turned the interview into requirements; three weeks later we wrote the second version with extra inputs we got from the site visit.\n\n! Requirement | First definition (Jan 23) | Second definition (Feb 14)\nFriction | not so slippery the user falls, not so rough it hurts skin | unchanged\nStability | no fear of falling; a handle if the board shifts | unchanged\nLoad | holds 500 lb | holds 200 lb\nLength | 3 ft at most | 3 ft at most\nWater | survives frequent contact | unchanged\nCleaning | antiseptic | dropped\nCost | $60 at most | $55 at most", "layout": "facts", "media": []}, {"id": "brainstorm", "stage": "brainstorm", "title": "We broke our own moving-parts rule", "text": "We each filled a row of an alternatives matrix. Mine asked for a slick, skin-friendly top over a metal structure “that allows the sliding to happen”, with rubber underneath; teammates added a tapered edge, a grab hole, a silk cover and rubber ends. I led the mockup plan: four tests on a standard board (silk cover, hollow handles, rubber bottom, slanted side), each tried with and without. After the site visit we split over two new options. Our instructors liked the dissent and reminded us DTC is a design class, not a prototype class. We chose the sliding seat, though our own constraints said “as little moving parts as possible”.", "layout": "gallery", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/slidemaster/mockup-plan-combined-sketch.webp", "caption": "“A sketch of our design” from the mockup plan (Jan 25, 2024): slanted side, silk transfer surface, hollow handles, rubber bottom.", "kind": "sketch"}, {"src": "assets/projects/slidemaster/mockup-plan-pillowcase.webp", "caption": "Mockup 1: a silk “pillowcase” pulled over a standard board.", "kind": "sketch"}, {"src": "assets/projects/slidemaster/mockup-plan-hollow-handles.webp", "caption": "Mockup 2: hollow handles at both ends for a better grip.", "kind": "sketch"}, {"src": "assets/projects/slidemaster/mockup-plan-rubber-bottom.webp", "caption": "Mockup 3: rubber on the underside, maybe the two ends too, to grip the chair and the tub.", "kind": "sketch"}, {"src": "assets/projects/slidemaster/mockup-plan-slanted-side.webp", "caption": "Mockup 4: a slanted end to scoop under the patient.", "kind": "sketch"}]}, {"id": "prototype", "stage": "prototype", "title": "Failures and Iterations", "text": "We cut the first base from plywood and sanded its offcuts to 10°, 20° and 30° tapers, measured from the bottom and from the middle, then tested them for impact and tension; 10° from the bottom won. On February 13 a teammate tried a wheelchair-to-shower-chair transfer on the board. He never put his full weight down: the plywood began to fracture. We moved to solid wood, sat on the candidates ourselves and settled on pine. I helped build our Tinkercad model and draw the orthographic views, and at the design review three days later I presented the designs we had dropped and why. Reviewers asked for a much wider seat and softer edges; afterwards we traded wheels in rails for ball-bearing drawer slides.", "layout": "gallery", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/slidemaster/tinkercad-early-model.webp", "caption": "Early Tinkercad model: hollow centre, rails for wheels, a sloped edge, a seat on top.", "kind": "cad"}, {"src": "assets/projects/slidemaster/design-review-old-design.webp", "caption": "My drawing of an earlier 30 × 10-inch version in top, front and side views, from my design-review slide.", "kind": "sketch"}, {"src": "assets/projects/slidemaster/design-review-sketch.webp", "caption": "The board as we showed it at the design review (Feb 16, 2024): slanted end, hollow centre, a seat on rails.", "kind": "sketch"}, {"src": "assets/projects/slidemaster/design-freeze-dimensions.webp", "caption": "Dimension drawing from the design-freeze package: 29 × 9-inch base, 23 × 3-inch cavity, 10 × 11-inch seat, 9 × 4-inch rubber end.", "kind": "sketch"}]}, {"id": "final", "stage": "final", "title": "The seat slides so the skin doesn't", "text": "Slide Master is a 29 × 9 × ¾-inch pine board; a 23 × 3-inch cavity cuts weight and doubles as a handle. A tapered end slips under the thigh, and rubber under the other end grips the tub. Two 12-inch ball-bearing drawer slides carry a cutting-board seat that covers them, so skin rides on the seat instead of dragging or pinching. We conducted the demo with students and faculty at the Segal Design Institute, along with our project partners, on March 9. Participants sat on the device and experienced the transfer process firsthand. We were pleased to see that the system worked successfully across users of different ages, genders, body sizes, and weights.\n", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/slidemaster/final-board-test-setup.webp", "caption": "The finished board between a shower chair and a wheelchair, in the test corner we set up at the Ford Design Center.", "kind": "photo"}, {"src": "assets/projects/slidemaster/exploded-view.webp", "caption": "The exploded view I drew for our poster: seat, slides, hollowed base, tapered end, rubber strip.", "kind": "sketch"}]}, {"id": "next", "stage": "next", "title": "Next: thinner, cheaper, easier to clean", "text": "Our report's list for the next version starts with testing by the people it's for and bringing the height down. It also proposes a different mechanism: a slide rail running in a U-channel cut into the board, which would hide the moving parts, give the seat a natural mount and reduce the pinch point that manufacturing errors could create. ", "layout": "text", "media": []}]}, "yello": {"slug": "yello", "name": "CASE × Yello", "floor": "analytics", "onSite": true, "order": 2, "template": "analytics", "status": "approved", "oneLiner": "Where a campus-recruiting software company could grow by partnering instead of competing.", "context": {"course": "", "when": "Fall 2024", "team": "Six analysts (five on the final deck), two project managers", "partner": "Yello, through CASE (Consulting Advising Student Enterprise)", "role": "Strategy Analyst — partnerships & expansion"}, "software": ["slides"], "skills": [{"name": "Problem framing", "evidence": "question"}, {"name": "Secondary research", "evidence": "approach"}, {"name": "Competitive analysis", "evidence": "analysis"}, {"name": "Posters & presentations", "evidence": "recommendation"}], "classes": [], "cover": "assets/projects/yello.webp", "blocks": [{"id": "question", "stage": "question", "title": "Map the market, then pick Yello's partners", "text": "Yello sells recruiting software built around early talent: the tools employers use at a campus career fair to capture candidates, schedule interviews and keep in touch. In fall 2024 it asked CASE, a Northwestern student consulting group, to map the HR-tech ecosystem it plays in, commercial and federal, and to recommend where it could partner, including the size of each opportunity.\n\nWe split into two workstreams. I was one of three analysts on partnership identification and expansion; the other three mapped competitors.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/yello/project-overview.webp", "caption": "The project on one slide: client background and our two workstreams (final deck).", "kind": "screen"}]}, {"id": "context", "stage": "context", "title": "Yello works beside the ATS, not against it", "text": "In the commercial market Yello isn't the system of record. It plugs into the big applicant tracking systems (ATS) and handles the top of the funnel. At our first client meeting the message was plain: the goal is not to replace Workday but to be used alongside an ATS, and a complementary partner is the easiest path to growth.\n\nYello also holds FedRAMP authorization, which lets it sell to federal agencies, and the brief asked for the federal market to be mapped on its own.", "layout": "text", "media": []}, {"id": "data", "stage": "data", "title": "Public research, estimated numbers, no client data", "text": "We had no client data to crunch. The deck rests on secondary research: Gartner's market guide for recruiting technology, Deloitte and McKinsey reports, J.P. Morgan equity research on Workday, PitchBook SaaS figures, Federal Reserve employment data, and Yello's own sales deck and campus-recruiting report.\n\nThat set the limits. Market shares came partly from vendor blogs, and every valuation in the deck is an estimate. My own starting file was simpler: the list of 24 companies Yello already partnered with.", "layout": "text", "media": []}, {"id": "approach", "stage": "approach", "title": "Start from the partners Yello already has", "text": "The ecosystem team profiled competitors, one company each. On our side, each analyst brought one candidate and had to justify it. I started from Yello's existing partners — ATS vendors, assessment tools, integration firms — and looked for what was missing around campus recruiting.\n\nMy pick was Handshake. Yello's own blog says most of its customers already use Handshake alongside Yello, so a formal tie would follow behaviour that was already there. Teammates took the public sector: government internship channels and a defense contractor.", "layout": "text", "media": []}, {"id": "analysis", "stage": "analysis", "title": "The big market is locked; the middle isn't", "text": "The ecosystem team's numbers framed everything. Over 98.8% of Fortune 500 companies already use an ATS, with Workday at 37.1% and SuccessFactors at 13.1%, and high switching costs leave little room there for a small vendor. The mid-market is fragmented and integration-friendly: Greenhouse, its largest player at 19.3%, offers over 450 pre-built integrations, Yello's among them. Beside its closest CRM rivals Yello was the small one, at an estimated $136M valuation to Beamery's $1B. Growth meant plugging in, not displacing.", "layout": "text-media", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/yello/crm-alternatives.webp", "caption": "Yello against its closest CRM alternatives (ecosystem workstream's slide).", "kind": "screen"}, {"src": "assets/projects/yello/mid-market-ats.webp", "caption": "The mid-market: fragmented, and built on integrations (ecosystem workstream's slide).", "kind": "screen"}]}, {"id": "recommendation", "stage": "recommendation", "title": "Partner sideways, and use the federal badge", "text": "We recommended partnerships over head-on competition. Service peers: Handshake for its campus network, RippleMatch for AI-driven sourcing, Pymetrics for game-based soft-skill assessments that support fairer hiring. Federal: GoGovernment, which gathers federal internships in one place, and Leidos, the defense industry's largest IT services provider, where Yello's FedRAMP authorization counts.\n\nA teammate's comment in the final week sharpened the pitch: stop measuring Yello against Workday, and present it as a mid-market tool that works alongside the big suites.", "layout": "gallery", "media": [{"src": "assets/projects/yello/partner-peers.webp", "caption": "Three service peers to partner with (final deck, slide 15).", "kind": "screen"}, {"src": "assets/projects/yello/government-channels.webp", "caption": "The public-sector channels (teammate's slide).", "kind": "screen"}, {"src": "assets/projects/yello/key-findings.webp", "caption": "Synthesis: the three takeaways we closed on.", "kind": "screen"}]}, {"id": "next", "stage": "next", "title": "We named partners but never sized them", "text": "The brief asked for the size of each opportunity, and our deck never gave one. The only hard number on the partnership slide — RippleMatch freeing up to 25% of recruiter time and $50,000 a year — has no source behind it, and first reached review typed as \"$5,0000\". Pymetrics, meanwhile, was already on the partner list I had compiled, so the slide should have said whether we meant a new tie or a deeper one. Next time I'd size each partner, even roughly, from shared customers or event volume.", "layout": "text", "media": []}]}};
